<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= this.size() => \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size())-1; (this.get(i) == null => this.get(i+1) == null) && (this.get(i) != null => this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null ? this.get(i+1) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i-1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null && \old(this.get(i-1)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

However, the above assert statement is incorrect based on the given natural language specification. The correct interpretation should be:

For every element from the index to the old size minus one, the element at the current position in the new list should be equal to the element at the previous position in the old list.

Thus, the correct assert statement should be:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

But since the element at the index position itself will change and the correct statement should reflect that the element at the next position is equal to the current element before the insertion, the correct assert statement should be:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index<i && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This ensures that from the index+1 position to the old size minus one, the elements remain the same as they were before the insertion.
